module test.claim.named_argument_binding_two_route_witness_test

import std.types { String, Bool, Int }
import v2.compiler.compile { compile_dag_source_to_target_text }
import v2.std.diagnostic { Accepted, Rejected }
import gunbc.compile_census_probe { total_blocking_count_for }

// ONE FIXTURE SOURCE, TWO COMPILERS, ONE QUESTION: which argument feeds which parameter. The two
// routes answer it differently, and the disagreement is about whether the children's SEQUENCE is
// part of the answer.
//
// WHAT THE MODEL SAYS, which is what makes one of the two answers wrong rather than merely
// different. A function's parameter set is the Arrow's DOMAIN, a `TypeNode { connective: Conj }`
// whose children carry `Edge { label: Named { name } }`. `v2.std.node` `connective_edge_discipline`
// gives Conj `LabeledEdges`, and `canonicalize_labeled_for_content_hash` SORTS LabeledEdges before
// hashing -- so a parameter list has no identity-bearing sequence. The parameter set is name-keyed,
// and the NAME is the binding key. The application is the other side: `behavior_edge_discipline`
// gives `Transform` `PositionalEdges`, which is not sorted, so it carries an ORDER.
//
// WHERE v2 ANSWERS FROM THE ORDER. `v2.compiler.body_lowering_fold` `body_lower_named_arg_value_optional`
// decodes the `name ^dag_token_colon value` spine, matches the parameter binding as `_binding`, and
// returns only the operand; `body_lower_transform_call` then builds one `Edge { label: Positional }`
// per argument. Downstream, `v2.compiler.infer` `infer_application_argument_inhabitance` recovers
// formals with `infer_formals_from_domain` (the Conj's STORED order) and arguments with
// `infer_transform_argument_targets` (stored order) and ZIPS them by index. So the same compiler
// treats the Conj's stored order as a fact in one fold and sorts it away for identity in another.
//
// WHERE v1 ANSWERS FROM THE NAME, on BOTH of its routes. `v1.compiler.emit`
// `order_typed_call_args_from_semantics` selects each argument by `matched_argument_index` from the
// `application_plan` that `v1.compiler.infer` built by parameter identity, and renders arguments in
// FORMAL order. And `v1_compiler.interpreter` `eval_call` binds a LABELLED actual by its name after
// `param_name_matches_declared`, keying only an UNLABELLED actual by position; what it walks in
// stored order is EVALUATION, which is a different fact from binding. That route is already
// fail-closed on every conflict the mixed form can produce -- an unknown label, a duplicate label, a
// positional actual landing on a slot a named actual already bound, a surplus positional -- so the
// refusal behaviour a name-bound v2 would need is an authority that already exists.
//
// THE ARMS. `nab_inorder_source` and `nab_reordered_source` differ in NOTHING but the order the two
// named arguments are written in, and `nab_pick` declares them at DIFFERENT types (`a: Int`,
// `b: Bool`) so a position-keyed binding is type-discriminating rather than silent. The in-order
// pair is the positive control: both routes must compile it. The out-of-order pair is the
// discriminating fixture, and its two arms are what the divergence IS.
//
// ONE ARM PER ROUTE PER FIXTURE, AND THE SPLIT IS NOT COSMETIC (review 70029). A conjunction of the
// two positive controls made one budget pay a full interpreted `compile_dag_source_to_target_text`
// AND a seed census compile, and -- worse for a file whose entire subject is that two routes
// disagree -- a red would not have said WHICH route regressed. Splitting them loses no coverage,
// which is the test DESIGN section 3's witness rule states for exactly this shape.
//
// THE v1 COUNT IS ASKED AS A TOTAL, NOT PER CLASS. `total_blocking_count_for` returns -1 when the
// census is not runnable, so a census that never ran cannot read as a clean compile; and asking for
// the TOTAL rather than for one wanted class means an UPSTREAM refusal cannot satisfy the arm by
// leaving the class it was looking for at zero.
//
// THE v2 ARM IS A KNOWN-HOLE PROBE, DESIGN section 4b(4), AND WHAT IT ASSERTS IS THE DEFECT RATHER
// THAN THE CONTRACT. Read that sentence before citing this file. The arm
// `the_v2_route_binds_out_of_declaration_order_by_position_which_is_the_defect` is GREEN TODAY
// BECAUSE v2 IS WRONG: it refuses a call the source wrote correctly, and the arm records that
// refusal. It is not a statement that v2 may refuse such a call, and it may not be cited as
// establishing the binding contract -- the contract is the opposite, and it is stated above: the
// NAME is the binding authority and positional sequence is derived. The arm FLIPS when the repair
// lands rather than retiring, at which point it inverts into the permanent regression control that
// the binding is by name. An arm asserting the right answer would simply be red and would carry the
// same information less honestly; an arm whose text did not say which of its two readings is the
// defect is how a hole calcifies into a cited behaviour.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH. It measures the SOURCE-to-refusal path on v2 and the source-to-
// diagnostic-census path on v1. It does not compare emitted bytes, and it says nothing about which
// of the 171 corpus call sites written out of declaration order would change binding -- that census
// is in the pull request that carried this file, derived by a stated method over the corpus rather
// than by an enrolled instrument, and it is a reading rather than a wall.
//
// THE CLASS is `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.an_application_binds_by_position_where_the_model_binds_by_name`,
// whose next-rung trigger names the capability: the lowered application carries the argument NAME on
// the carrier that owns the binding, and every consumer reads the binding from that one place.

data nab_inorder_source: String = "module nab_inorder\n\nfn nab_pick(a: Int, b: Bool) -> Int \{\n  a\n}\n\nfn nab_use() -> Int \{\n  nab_pick(a: 1, b: true)\n}\n"

data nab_reordered_source: String = "module nab_reordered\n\nfn nab_pick(a: Int, b: Bool) -> Int \{\n  a\n}\n\nfn nab_use() -> Int \{\n  nab_pick(b: true, a: 1)\n}\n"

fn nab_v2_accepts(source: String) -> Bool {
  match compile_dag_source_to_target_text(source_text: source) {
    Accepted { value: _, diagnostics: _ } => true
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => false
  }
}

test fn the_v2_route_accepts_the_declaration_order_call_which_is_its_positive_control() -> Bool {
  nab_v2_accepts(source: nab_inorder_source)
}

test fn the_v1_routes_accept_the_declaration_order_call_which_is_their_positive_control() -> Bool {
  total_blocking_count_for(source: nab_inorder_source) == 0
}

test fn the_v1_routes_bind_out_of_declaration_order_by_name_which_is_the_contract() -> Bool {
  total_blocking_count_for(source: nab_reordered_source) == 0
}

test fn the_v2_route_binds_out_of_declaration_order_by_position_which_is_the_defect() -> Bool {
  !nab_v2_accepts(source: nab_reordered_source)
}
